# 2. The package is built by tsc, resolved as nodenext

Date: {{ date }}

## Status

Accepted

## Context

This repository publishes a TypeScript package. The obvious build is the one
every application in the workspace already uses - Vite, in library mode - and
the obvious `moduleResolution` is `bundler`, which is what the repository's
own tooling wants.

Both are wrong for a published package, and wrong in a way that passes every
check. Under `bundler`, an extensionless relative specifier - `./greet` -
typechecks; `tsc` emits it unchanged; the package builds, packs and publishes.
Node's ESM resolver then refuses it, because it requires the extension. The
failure belongs entirely to whoever installs the package.

## Decision

The package is compiled by `tsc` with `module` and `moduleResolution` set to
`nodenext`, and its sources import each other with the `.js` extension the
emitted files will have.

The workspace's own `tsconfig.base.json` keeps `bundler`, which is right for
anything a bundler consumes. The published package overrides it, because it is
consumed by Node.

No bundler in the package build: there is nothing to bundle. A library ships
modules, and a consumer's bundler does the bundling with far better
information than this repository has.

## Consequences

- Relative imports inside the package carry `.js`, which reads oddly next to a
  `.ts` file and is correct: the extension names the emitted file.
- CI installs the packed tarball into a scratch directory and imports it with a
  plain `node`, outside the workspace. Typechecking cannot catch this class -
  it is the resolver that disagrees - so a different process has to try.
- `exports` names the entry points explicitly. A module not listed there is
  private however it is laid out under `dist`.
